"""
SOT-223 linear regulator.

Hand-written part definitions. The footprint file beside this one is the KiCad
library `SOT223`, which atopile registers from this directory's name. Datasheet
revision and the pin mapping checked against it are recorded in SOT223.md.

Each part asserts its own datasheet values, so a board that asks for something
this part cannot do resolves to an `<empty>` spec, which the build gate rejects.
"""


import is_atomic_part
import has_designator_prefix
import has_part_picked

component Ldo3v3:
    """
    Gainsil GS2401C-33 LDO. SOT-223: 1 = GND, 2 = VOUT (tab), 3 = VIN.

    Not the AMS1117 first drawn. That part's absolute maximum input is 15 V,
    below the 19.9 V the input TVS clamps at, so during a surge the regulator
    rather than the TVS would have been the first thing to fail. A 12 V
    stand-off TVS cannot clamp below 15 V, so the regulator had to move.

    This one survives 44 V and *operates* to 40 V, so a clamp event leaves it
    inside its normal range rather than merely alive. Quiescent current also
    falls from about 5 mA to 1.3 uA, which on a rail this small is most of the
    board's idle draw.

    Kept as a plain component: the standard Regulator type models a different
    shape, and the part number is this device's identity anyway.

    The symbol is KiCad's LM1084-3.3. No symbol exists for this part, and that
    one carries the same pin numbering. atopile renders no schematic, so the
    symbol is recorded metadata only.
    """
    trait is_atomic_part<manufacturer="Gainsil", partnumber="GS2401C-33CTR3", footprint="SOT-223-3_TabPin2.kicad_mod", symbol="Regulator_Linear:LM1084-3.3">
    trait has_designator_prefix<prefix="U">
    trait has_part_picked<manufacturer="Gainsil", partno="GS2401C-33CTR3", supplier_partno="C6283798", supplier_id="lcsc">
    v_in_max: V
    assert v_in_max within 44V
    v_out: V
    assert v_out within 3.3V +/- 1%

    signal gnd ~ pin 1
    signal vout ~ pin 2
    signal vin ~ pin 3
